    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>What do recruiters look at when a student has no work experience?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">Recruiters are looking for one thing first: a fast, credible signal that you can do the work. When you have no work history to read, most applications get only seconds and fall back on weak proxies like school name. The thing that actually stops a recruiter is evaluated proof of work, something they can judge directly rather than take on faith.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>With no experience to read, recruiters default to proxies (school, keywords, GPA) and most applications get only a few seconds.</li>
        <li>Those proxies are weakening: <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers now use skills-based hiring</a>, prioritizing demonstrated ability (TestGorilla, 2025).</li>
        <li>The strongest signal a no-experience candidate can give is a real, evaluated work sample in the target role.</li>
        <li>Evidence beats claims because a recruiter can judge it directly and quickly, instead of inferring from a list.</li>
        <li>This matters because the stakes are real: about <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">41.5% of recent graduates are underemployed</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What happens when a student applies with no experience?</h2>
      <p>Be clear-eyed about the mechanics, because they are not flattering. A recruiter facing a large applicant pool spends only seconds on each résumé in the first pass, and software often screens applications before a human sees them. For a candidate with real work history, those seconds land on accomplishments. For a student with none, they land on proxies: which school, what GPA, which keywords matched the posting. None of those prove you can do the job. They are just the least-bad guesses available when there is no evidence to read, which is why so many strong students get filtered out before anyone forms a real opinion of them.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What signal actually stops a recruiter?</h2>
      <p>Something they can evaluate. The reason proxies dominate is not that recruiters love them, it is that nothing better is usually in the file. Put a real, evaluated piece of work in front of them and the calculus changes. A recruiter can open a scored marketing brief or product spec and form a genuine judgment in roughly the same few seconds they would have spent skimming bullets, except now they are judging the work itself. That shift, from being inferred to being evaluated, is the entire game for a no-experience candidate, because evaluation is where good work wins and a thin résumé loses.</p>
      <p>The market is moving this way on its own. Skills-based hiring rose to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers in 2025</a> while résumé reliance fell, which means more recruiters than ever are actively looking for demonstrated ability rather than pedigree. You are not fighting the trend by leading with proof. You are giving recruiters exactly the signal they are increasingly trying to find.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you build that signal as a student?</h2>
      <p>Produce one real thing and get it judged. Pick the role you want, do an actual task from it, and have the result evaluated against a professional standard so its quality is established rather than asserted. While building zero, we ran a Middle East university pilot in Spring 2026, and the pattern that stood out was how much students valued specific feedback on the work they had just done over generic advice. That feedback is also what makes a work sample credible to a recruiter: a score from someone qualified to give it turns your work from something you made into something that has been vetted. Build a couple of those in your target field and you walk into every application with the one thing the file usually lacks. zero is built to produce exactly this, scored work that becomes a receipt recruiters can evaluate, free for students because recruiters pay for the access.</p>
    </section>

